ther's experience  over the last year  but also develop a shared understanding  of where our priorities need to be  in the future how can we use the broad  shoulders of the uk treasury  in order to ensure that uh there is the  right investment  in the nhs and scotland and the right  support for scottish students to catch  up on their education  um let's talk a bit about the uh what  happens next with the recovery from the  pandemic because we're told that the  queen's speech is going to be focusing  on  you know building back one of the areas  that has been  really hit hard by kevin 19 is care  homes  an area of course where the the  difficulties  in that particular sector have also been  very much exposed  by the pandemic too now when he became  prime minister  boris johnson said i'm announcing now on  the steps of downing street that we  fixed the crisis in social care once and  for all with a clear plan we have  prepared  now this mysterious clear plan seems  like it's never really appeared are we  going to finally get a glimpse of it  yes we'll be saying more about social  care in the weeks and months to come  you're absolutely right it's a it's a  huge issue um uh  with an aging population with uh  the particular challenges that uh  dementia alzheimer's and  and other conditions pose we do need to  make sure that we improve the operation  of our social care system  we also know that while there are some  outstanding care providers  people like robert kilgauer and others  it's also the case  uh that there are some settings which do  not maintain the high standards that  we'd want  so we need to look at funding we need to  look at an inspection  we need to look at how we can make sure  that we get the investment required  we need to look at how we can improve  quality and helen waitley the care  minister and matt hancock the secretary  of state  have been developing a plan on this and  we'll be saying more in due course  absolutely  you say say more in due course